  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development would result in the replacement of a large single family dwelling with self-contained apartments in a part of the road where one out of the consecutive row of five dwellings has already undergone such a development. This would result in the erosion of the established character of the immediate area and together with the consequential scale, bulk, massing and site coverage of the new development, would detract from the quality of the street scene and the character of the area. The proposal is therefore contrary to Section 5 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies DMD5 of the Development Management Document (2014) and HW-1 and HW-11 of the Hadley Wood Neighbourhood Plan (2023).",
    "The proposed development, by reason of sitting, scale and mass would create a cramped and appear visually intrusive, incongruous, overly dominant development, out of keeping with the character, development pattern and appearance of development along Beech Hill. The proposal is contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D3, D4, D5, D8 of the London Plan (2021), CP30 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD6, DMD8 and DMD37 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and HW-12 of the Hadley Wood Neighbourhood Plan (2023).",
    "The proposed two houses, by reason of failure to provide adequate private amenity space on site would provide a substandard level of residential occupation for future occupiers of the residential developments. As such the proposal would be contrary to Section 12 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies D6 of the London Plan (2021), DMD9 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, in the absence of adequate justification to demonstrate safe and shared pedestrian access commensurate with the use proposed, would give rise to unsafe pedestrian movements, contrary to Section 9 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies T2, T3 and T4 of the London Plan (2021) CP25 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD45 and DMD47 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed two houses by reason of the failure to provide adequate and satisfactory refuse access and storage for future occupiers of the development would result in compromised servicing of the site. As such the development is contrary to Section 9 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies T7 of the London Plan (2021), DMD8 and DMD47 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014), Household recycling and waste planning guidance for new developments (2024).",
    "In the absence of a legal agreement to secure an appropriate carbon offset contribution, the proposal would not offer a mechanism to offset the shortfall of meeting net-zero carbon and fail to achieve an acceptable level of greenhouse gases reduction. The proposal would therefore contrary to Policy SI2 of the London Plan (2021) and Policy DMD51 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The proposed development, by virtue of the loss of  trees including a tree subject of a Tree Preservation Order and insufficient details and measures to protect valued trees, would be detrimental to the visual amenity and biodiversity value within the site and the surrounding area, contrary to Section 15 of the National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies G6 and G7 of the London Plan (2021), CP34 and CP36 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), DMD37, DMD 80 and DMD81 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and HW-7 of the Hadley Wood Neighbourhood Plan (2023)."
  ],
